\import{ftip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A paired trace-selection calculation}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{evaluation}

\p{The selective-refinement comparison motivates contrasting repeatability and
coverage under a fixed retained budget
\citet{Sections 6-7; Tables 4-5, 10}{dang2026selecting}. Consider two arms from
\ref{ftip-00P8} with the same P0 boundary, model,
task split, verifier, budget, and paired randomization law. They differ only
in the selector: #{a} retains the highest teacher-forced scores and #{a'}
retains a coverage-stratified subset. Both have
#{Q_{\rm hold}=\{q_1,q_2\}}, #{\mathfrak S=\{s_1,s_2\}}, and #{N=2}. Suppose
the admitted receipts are}
##{
\begin{array}{c|cc|cc}
 &q_{a,i}&s_{a,i}&I^{\rm prod}_{a,i}&Y_{a,i}\\\hline
i=1&q_1&s_1&1&1\\
i=2&q_1&s_1&1&0
\end{array}
\qquad
\begin{array}{c|cc|cc}
 &q_{a',i}&s_{a',i}&I^{\rm prod}_{a',i}&Y_{a',i}\\\hline
i=1&q_1&s_1&1&1\\
i=2&q_2&s_2&1&1
\end{array}
}
\p{Then both arms have
#{\widehat p^{\rm prod}=1}, while
#{\widehat C^{\rm task}_a=\widehat C^{\rm strat}_a=\tfrac12} and
#{\widehat C^{\rm task}_{a'}=\widehat C^{\rm strat}_{a'}=1}. The paired
outcome contrast is #{\widehat\Delta_{a,a'}=-\tfrac12}. The calculation
shows why a repeatability rate alone cannot identify coverage: the two
selectors have the same productive rate but different admitted task and
strategy support. This is a finite FTIP construction using the receipt
definitions, not a source result.}
